# memdb memory-layer architecture (current, 2026-08-31)

Reference for any agent session on this project. memdb is the cross-host,
log-first memory system sessions record into and read from. Everything
below is generic: it assumes one or more machines ("hosts"), each with a
short slug; nothing depends on any particular fleet.

## 1. Design: log-first, projection-derived

- SYSTEM OF RECORD: a per-host, append-only, hash-chained event journal
  at `<journal-root>/journal/<host>/events.jsonl` (default root
  `~/.claude/memory/journal`, override `$MEMDB_JOURNAL`). Every event is
  one canonical JSON line carrying `(v, host, seq, ts, type, prev,
  payload)`: `seq` is a per-host monotonic counter, `prev` is the
  SHA-256 of the previous event's canonical line, so truncation,
  reordering, or mutation is mechanically detectable.
- PROJECTION: the SQLite DB (`$MEMDB_PATH`, default
  `~/.claude/memory/mem.db`) is DISPOSABLE. `rebuild` reproduces it from
  journals alone; `verify` proves the live DB equals a canonical replay
  and every chain is intact. Losing or corrupting the DB loses nothing.
  Never edit journal files or the DB by hand.
- Host identity: `~/.claude/memory/host-id` (or `$MEMDB_HOST`); writes
  refuse to run without it.
- Canonical event order across hosts: sort by (running-max ts per host,
  host, seq). The running-max key means an event never sorts before its
  own host's predecessors even if its wall-clock ts is older.
- Stable addressing: uid `<host>:<seq>` (episode children
  `<host>:<seq>/<n>`). Local integer ids are reassigned by every rebuild
  and must never be used across sessions.
- Concurrency: one cross-process file lock per journal root serializes
  all writes (append and DB apply happen under the same lock hold);
  reads (digest, search, get) are lock-free by contract - session start
  must never block behind a rebuild. The projection swap is a single
  atomic replace, retried on Windows sharing violations.

## 2. Tool

`<memdb-repo>/memdb/mem.py` — Python 3 stdlib only. Subcommands:
`init, add, get, search, supersede, close, record-episode,
render-handoff, digest, export, consolidate, rebuild, verify, snapshot,
doctor, redact-check`.

## 3. Data model (schema v3)

One `records` table; the `kind` column is the memory type:

- `episode` — one completed job (achievement / decisions / current_state
  / blockers / next_steps as JSON payload; raw text FTS-indexed at low
  rank weight). Episodes are born closed and are never superseded.
- `decision`, `blocker`, `state` — children auto-extracted from an
  episode doc by `record-episode`. Blockers whose subject slug appears
  in a later episode's achievements/state text auto-close, project-wide.
- `goal`, `constraint`, `preference` — durable semantic anchors. The
  `core-setup` part holds session-start invariants (tool paths, hazards,
  standing preferences); project `global` applies everywhere.
- `runbook` — a proven-good command for a task: `subject` = task slug,
  `body` = the exact command that ran successfully, `rationale` =
  cwd/date/notes.

Write gating (`add` and episode children): an open record with the same
`(project, kind, subject)` and same normalized body -> re-confirm (bump
`last_confirmed_at`); different body -> insert new and supersede all
open matches (link row records the supersede edge); no match -> insert.
Status lifecycle: `open -> closed | superseded`. Gating matches project
CASE-SENSITIVELY (replay determinism); read paths are case-insensitive
(section 6). FTS5 over subject/body/rationale/fulltext, bm25 weights
5/1/1/0.5.

## 4. Fast-path machinery (scalability layer, 2026-08-31)

All fast paths are disposable caches over the journal; every one
self-heals or falls back LOUDLY to the slow, always-correct path.

- HEAD SIDECAR (`<journal-root>/derived/<host>/head.json`): O(1) append.
  Stores `{seq, hash, start, end}` of the tail line; validated by
  re-reading and re-hashing exactly those bytes before trust; any
  mismatch -> one full rescan. Written after the fsynced append, so a
  crash between the two leaves a detectably-stale sidecar, never a
  wrong seq/prev. `derived/` lives OUTSIDE `journal/` so replication
  can never pick it up.
- VERIFIED-HEAD ANCHORS (`derived/<host>/verified-head.json`): suffix
  verification. Chain walks start from the last VERIFIED head instead
  of seq 1; the anchor's bytes are re-validated first; stale/invalid ->
  full walk. Anchors advance only after a successful verify, never on
  failure. Trust boundary: tampering below a valid anchor is caught by
  the scheduled full walk and by witnesses.
- WITNESSES (`journal/<host>/witness-heads.json`): each host commits its
  verified view `{other-host: {seq, hash}}` on its own branch. During
  any chain walk, a traversed seq whose hash differs from a witnessed
  value fails verification ("split view or rewritten line") - the
  transparency-log witness property over the existing replication.
- SNAPSHOT (`mem.db.snapshot` + `.manifest.json`): O(tail) rebuild.
  The manifest records schema version, per-host tail anchors +
  running-max ts, the globally last merge key (`max_key`), and the
  snapshot file's SHA-256. Routine `rebuild`/`verify` copy the snapshot
  and replay only events past each host's anchor THROUGH THE SAME fold
  function a full replay uses - that identity is what keeps
  snapshot-derived state provably equal to full replay. Guards:
  schema mismatch / file-hash mismatch / stale anchor -> loud fallback
  to full replay; any tail event whose merge key sorts at/before
  `max_key` (a rewound clock would reorder history) -> loud fallback;
  chain damage -> FAILURE, never fallback. Every successful full replay
  refreshes the snapshot. `--full` on rebuild/verify forces the oracle.
- Verification comparison is streamed row-by-row over uid-ordered
  cursors (O(1) memory), not by building whole-DB dumps.

Measured envelope (100k events / ~105 MB journal / ~240 MB DB): append
~18 ms steady-state (one-time self-heal ~0.5 s), snapshot rebuild ~1 s
vs ~90 s full replay, suffix chain verify ~15 ms. Budgets enforced by
`tests/bench_scale.py`; see `docs/perf-budgets.md`.

## 5. Replication (any number of hosts)

Git, hub-and-spoke: the journal root is a git clone; each host commits
ONLY `journal/<host>/` on its own branch `<host>/journal` and pushes to
a private hub; other hosts' journals are materialized read-only from
origin refs, never merged. `sync/memdb_sync.sh` per run: commit own
journal -> push (never --force) -> fetch -> for every other host's ref,
assert FAST-FORWARD against the last-seen ref (a non-FF ref means a
rewritten journal or two writers claiming one host id: the sync STOPS
and reports, it never auto-resolves) -> materialize -> `rebuild
--verify` (snapshot fast path). On a cadence (default weekly, or
`MEMDB_FULL_VERIFY=1`) the full oracle runs instead: whole-chain walk +
from-scratch replay comparison, bounding every trust window. Shallow
clones are refused (cannot verify a chain below the shallow boundary).
Hub requirements (see `docs/hub-config.md`): `gc.pruneExpire` >= 2
weeks, no auto/aggressive gc racing pushes.

## 6. Retrieval

- `digest --project <slug> [--part <p>] [--budget-tokens N]` (default
  1500). PINNED, never trimmed: goals/constraints/preferences (project +
  global) and "Proven commands (run these first, exactly)" (runbooks,
  project-wide regardless of --part, newest confirmation first). If the
  pinned block alone reaches the budget, the budget extends by a fixed
  tail reserve so the handoff layer is never starved to zero. Trimmable
  tail, in order: Recent episodes (3, rendered FIRST - the strongest
  continuity signal), open blockers, live decisions, current state -
  capped at 15 items per section (newest first); every omission is
  counted in an explicit "[digest trimmed: N records omitted]" notice.
- `search --query ... [--project] [--part] [--kind] [--status]` emits
  summary lines (uid, subject, snippet); expand a promising hit with
  `get <uid>`. Never default to `--full`.
- Project matching on ALL read paths is case-insensitive (historical
  slug case-splits must not hide records); always WRITE the canonical
  slug. SQLite hazard for maintainers: `(col=? COLLATE NOCASE OR
  col='x')` silently loses its collation to the OR optimization
  (observed 3.50.4); use `col COLLATE NOCASE IN (...)`.
- SessionStart hook (`hooks/session_start.sh`): injects the digest for
  the project derived from cwd basename; never blocks session start;
  failure and emptiness are visible states, not silence.

## 7. Session workflow contract

- Task start: `digest --project <slug> --part core-setup` first, then
  the work part's digest; `search` for specific priors.
- Runbooks ("run exact command first"): after a command runs
  successfully, record it - `mem add --kind runbook --project <slug>
  --part <part> --subject "<task-slug>" --body "<exact command>"`.
  Re-recording the same command re-confirms it (floats up); a changed
  command supersedes the old one. A later session runs pinned runbooks
  FIRST, exactly as recorded, before deriving anything new.
- End of job: write the episode JSON and `record-episode`; durable
  facts stated mid-job get `add` (kind goal/constraint/preference,
  part core-setup) immediately. Close finished goals/blockers; never
  delete - supersede.
- Hygiene: `consolidate` merges duplicate open records and flags
  contradictions; `consolidate --expire-states DAYS` (opt-in) closes
  open `state` records unconfirmed in DAYS days - states only, by
  design: blockers never age out (age does not fix broken things) and
  decisions keep their rationale. All of it flows through journaled
  events, so it replays deterministically.
- Health: `doctor` (chains, heads, anchors, snapshot, FTS probe);
  `rebuild` is always safe (the DB is a projection).

## 8. Privacy model

Journals and DB are the PRIVATE memory domain (user machines + the
self-hosted hub): capture is full-fidelity, never pre-redacted.
Redaction guards the publish boundary at full strength: anything leaving
the private domain must pass `mem redact-check <file>` (exit 2 + span
report on hits). This file is itself redacted accordingly.
